{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: SCM providers without `getWorkflowRunJobs` skip the run-jobs lookup silently**

When `scm` is set but the provider doesn't implement `getWorkflowRunJobs`, `rawJobs` stays `null`, `ciModule._jobsOf(null)` returns `[]`, and the check gets no logs — with no log line at all. That's exactly the "silently skipped" symptom gh-982 set out to fix, just relocated. The duck-type guard is the right call for GitLab/ADO (their `details_url`s never match `/runs/N`), so this only bites a future/custom provider whose URLs do match — but a one-line `console.warn` in the `else` of the `typeof` check would keep the failure observable:

```javascript
if (typeof scm.getWorkflowRunJobs === 'function') {
    rawJobs = scm.getWorkflowRunJobs(runIdMatch[1]);
} else {
    console.warn('SCM provider has no getWorkflowRunJobs; skipping run-jobs lookup for', check.details_url);
}
```
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment1.md"}

{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: one job's log-fetch failure aborts the remaining jobs of the run**

The per-job loop has no inner try/catch: if `_fetchJobLogs(...)` throws for the first failed job (tool error, transient network failure), control jumps to the outer `catch` and the logs of all remaining failed jobs in the run are never fetched — the `ci_failures.md` for that check ends up with fewer logs than the run actually produced, and the outer warn doesn't say which job failed. Consider guarding each iteration:

```javascript
try {
    var jobLogs = _fetchJobLogs(scm, owner, repo, runJob.id);
    var jobLabel = runJob.name ? 'Error log (' + runJob.name + ')' : 'Error log';
    appendLog(check, jobLogs, jobLabel);
} catch (jobErr) {
    console.warn('Could not fetch logs for job', runJob.id, ':', jobErr.message || jobErr);
}
```

Best-effort tooling, so non-blocking — but per-job isolation matches the spirit of the `/job/M` path, where a fetch failure only ever costs that one job's log.
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment2.md"}

{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: drop the runtime-state churn from the PR**

This diff is fa-session bookkeeping (the live `running.json` bash-job registry), not PR content — it will conflict with every other agent branch touching the same file and has no functional value in the merge. Consider `git checkout origin/main -- .fah/bash_jobs/running.json` (or restoring it to main's version) before merge. Non-blocking; the file is already tracked on main, so this is noise reduction rather than a policy fix.
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment3.md"}

